export default JSON.parse("{\"4.5.1\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.1\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"A broker offers 500:1 leverage on EUR/USD to retail clients living in Romania. Is that allowed?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Yes, if you sign a waiver\",\"feedback\":\"No waiver lifts the EU retail cap. Only opting up to professional status, with strict criteria, removes it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"No: the EU retail cap on major FX pairs is 30:1\",\"feedback\":\"Right. An offer of 500:1 points to a firm outside EU rules.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Yes: leverage isn't regulated\",\"feedback\":\"In the EU it is: since 2018, retail CFD leverage has been capped by asset, 30:1 at most.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only on weekends\",\"feedback\":\"The cap applies at all times. A firm offering 500:1 to EU retail clients works outside EU rules.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. The EU retail cap on major pairs is 30:1. Offering 500:1 to a Romanian resident means a firm outside EU rules: a red flag (1.8).\",\"concept\":\"regulatory-perimeter\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Who supervises what · as of 22 Oct 2026\",\"prompt\":\"Match each regulator with what it supervises for retail traders.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"ASF\",\"right\":\"Romania: investment firms and the capital market\",\"wrong\":\"The [[asf|ASF]] is Romania's supervisor. It applies EU rules, including ESMA's retail measures.\"},{\"left\":\"FCA\",\"right\":\"UK: brokers, CFDs and spread betting\",\"wrong\":\"The [[fca|FCA]] supervises UK firms. Since Brexit, EU passports no longer reach the UK.\"},{\"left\":\"SEC and FINRA\",\"right\":\"US: securities and broker-dealers\",\"wrong\":\"The [[sec|SEC]] regulates US securities markets; [[finra|FINRA]] polices broker-dealers and their margin rules.\"},{\"left\":\"CFTC and NFA\",\"right\":\"US: futures and retail forex\",\"wrong\":\"US futures and retail forex fall under the [[cftc|CFTC]], with the [[nfa|NFA]] as the self-regulatory body.\"},{\"left\":\"ESMA\",\"right\":\"EU: coordinates the national regulators\",\"wrong\":\"[[esma|ESMA]] doesn't license brokers. It coordinates national authorities and can restrict products EU-wide, as it did with CFDs in 2018.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Regulator\",\"Supervises\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Each firm and product has a home [[regulator]]; that's the register you check.\",\"concept\":\"regulatory-perimeter\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"One licence, many countries\",\"text\":\"Under [[mifid-ii|MiFID II]], a firm licensed in one EU state can serve clients across the EU by [[passporting]]: a Cypriot firm can serve you in Romania. Find it on its home regulator's [[licence-register]], and the passport on the ASF's register. Offshore registrations give retail clients little protection: that's where 500:1 and bonuses live.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Retail protections · as of 22 Oct 2026\",\"text\":\"Since 2018, EU retail CFDs carry leverage caps from 30:1 down to 2:1, a close-out at 50% of required margin, [[negative-balance-protection]], no bonuses and a [[risk-warning]] with the share of losing accounts. Binary options are banned, and complex products need an [[appropriateness-test]]. The UK made similar rules permanent in 2019 and bans crypto CFDs for retail clients.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"The end of the PDT rule · as of 22 Oct 2026\",\"prompt\":\"A US client with 8,000 dollars in a margin account makes 5 day trades in 5 business days in October 2026. What applies?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Pattern day trader status: she needs 25,000 dollars\",\"feedback\":\"That was the rule until 3 June 2026. Some brokers may still apply it during the transition, so check your broker's terms.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Intraday margin: no 25,000 minimum, but deficits must be covered\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Day trading is banned below 25,000 dollars\",\"feedback\":\"It never was banned. The old rule restricted margin accounts under 25,000 dollars, and it has now been replaced.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"reveal\":\"From 4 June 2026, FINRA Rule 4210 has no [[pattern-day-trader-rule|pattern day trader]] section. Brokers track [[intraday-margin]] instead: the largest shortfall after a trade that adds risk is a deficit, to be met within 15 business days; small ones are exempt. Brokers may keep the old rule until 20 October 2027. Courses written before 2026 are out of date.\",\"concept\":\"us-intraday-margin\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Retail or professional\",\"text\":\"Under MiFID II, a [[retail-client]] gets every protection. You can be treated as a [[professional-client]] only by meeting two of three criteria: frequent large trades, a portfolio over €500,000, or a year working in finance. Opting up removes the leverage caps, negative balance protection and some warnings. A broker pushing you to opt up is selling risk.\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Verify a firm on the regulator's own [[licence-register]], never on the firm's website: the licence number, the exact legal entity, its permissions and its web addresses must all match. Clone firms copy real licence numbers. Rules here are as of 22 Oct 2026, reviewed yearly; next review October 2027.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Set the caps: EU retail\",\"prompt\":\"The simulator is set to EU retail. Match each product with its maximum leverage, then switch to a US retail client.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"EUR/USD CFD\",\"right\":\"30:1\",\"wrong\":\"EUR/USD is a major pair, which gets the highest cap: 30:1.\"},{\"left\":\"Gold CFD\",\"right\":\"20:1\",\"wrong\":\"Gold sits with non-major pairs and major indices at 20:1.\"},{\"left\":\"Single-stock CFD\",\"right\":\"5:1\",\"wrong\":\"Shares are capped at 5:1.\"},{\"left\":\"Bitcoin CFD\",\"right\":\"2:1\",\"wrong\":\"Crypto gets the lowest cap: 2:1.\"},{\"left\":\"Any CFD, US retail client\",\"right\":\"Not offered\",\"wrong\":\"CFDs aren't offered to US retail clients at all.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Product\",\"Maximum leverage\"],\"correct\":\"Right: 30, 20, 5 and 2, and no CFDs for US retail. Caps limit the largest position, not your risk: size from the stop.\",\"concept\":\"leverage\"}],\"check\":[\"q-4.5.1-1\",\"q-4.5.1-2\",\"q-4.5.1-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Each firm has a home regulator: ASF, FCA, SEC and FINRA, CFTC and NFA. Verify on its register.\",\"EU retail caps run from 30:1 to 2:1, with 50% close-out and negative balance protection. Date every rule.\",\"From 4 June 2026, US intraday margin replaced the pattern day trader rule; older courses are out of date.\"]}},\"4.5.2\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.2\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Your broker goes bust. Which of these is protected?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The trading losses you made last month\",\"feedback\":\"No scheme pays for trading losses. Schemes cover client money a failed firm can't return.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Cash at a US broker-dealer, up to $250,000\",\"feedback\":\"Right. SIPC covers up to $500,000 per customer, of which $250,000 in cash, if a broker-dealer fails.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Futures margin, under SIPC\",\"feedback\":\"SIPC covers securities accounts. Futures and forex fall outside it; segregation is their protection.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Coins left on a crypto exchange\",\"feedback\":\"Crypto that isn't a security sits outside SIPC and most schemes. That counterparty risk is yours.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. Only the cash at a US broker-dealer is covered, by SIPC, up to $250,000. Schemes pay when a firm fails, never for market losses.\",\"concept\":\"client-money-protection\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Segregation, then compensation · as of 22 Oct 2026\",\"text\":\"Regulated brokers hold your money as [[segregated-funds]], apart from their own. If a firm fails with money missing, an [[investor-compensation-scheme]] pays up to a limit: [[sipc|SIPC]] in the US, $500,000 including $250,000 cash; at least €20,000 in the EU; £85,000 from the UK's [[fscs|FSCS]]. Market losses are never covered. Check current limits.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"When client money failed\",\"prompt\":\"Real cases. Match each failure with what went wrong.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"MF Global, 2011\",\"right\":\"Client money used by the firm: segregation failed\",\"wrong\":\"MF Global's collapse left a shortfall in segregated client accounts: the money wasn't kept apart.\"},{\"left\":\"Peregrine Financial (PFG), 2012\",\"right\":\"The founder faked bank statements for years\",\"wrong\":\"At PFG the founder had forged the statements showing client money; segregation existed only on paper.\"},{\"left\":\"Alpari UK, 2015\",\"right\":\"Client losses after the Swiss franc jump made it insolvent\",\"wrong\":\"When the Swiss franc floor was removed, client losses beyond their deposits sank Alpari UK the next day.\"},{\"left\":\"FTX, 2022\",\"right\":\"Customer funds mixed with a sister firm: a hole of about $8 billion\",\"wrong\":\"FTX moved customer funds to its sister trading firm, leaving a gap of about $8 billion.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Case\",\"What went wrong\"],\"correct\":\"Right. In each case the firm, not the market, was the risk: [[counterparty-risk]].\",\"concept\":\"counterparty-risk\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Who's on the other side\",\"prompt\":\"Match each dealing model with how it earns and where the conflict sits.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"A-book: STP or ECN\",\"right\":\"Passes your order to outside liquidity; earns commission or a markup\",\"wrong\":\"An [[a-book]] broker routes your order on. It earns on your activity, not on your losses.\"},{\"left\":\"B-book: market maker\",\"right\":\"Takes the other side: your losses are its revenue, steady winners a liability\",\"wrong\":\"A [[b-book]] broker is your counterparty, so it gains when you lose.\"},{\"left\":\"Hybrid\",\"right\":\"Profiles clients and passes the winners to the market\",\"wrong\":\"A [[hybrid-broker]] keeps most clients in-house and routes consistent winners out.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Model\",\"How it earns\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Every model earns somewhere; the B-book earns where you lose.\",\"concept\":\"broker-models\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Same 50 trades, two brokers\",\"lines\":[\"Your 50 trades make +750.00 vUSD before costs.\",\"STP broker: 7.00 commission plus a 2.00 raw spread a trade, 450.00 in all. You net +300.00; it keeps 350.00 of commission whatever you make.\",\"B-book broker: a wider spread, 12.00 a trade, 600.00 in all. You net +150.00, and as your counterparty it loses those 150.00.\",\"Another client nets −400.00 at the same B-book broker. The broker's result = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":400,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":-400,\"text\":\"That's the client's result. The broker is the other side, so the sign flips.\"},{\"value\":600,\"text\":\"600.00 is the spread. As counterparty, the broker also pays the client's gross gain of 200.00: 600.00 − 200.00.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: +400.00. Under a B-book your loss is the broker's gain, and your gain its loss.\"},\"after\":\"Neither model is a scam by itself. An STP broker earns on your activity; a B-book broker earns on your losses. Many B-book brokers hedge their net exposure, but the incentive stays.\",\"concept\":\"broker-models\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"A [[b-book]] isn't automatically stop hunting, and an A-book label doesn't guarantee good fills. Judge execution, disclosure and controls. [[asymmetric-slippage|Asymmetric slippage]], where only bad slippage reaches you, is a red flag. [[last-look|Last look]] in FX falls under the FX Global Code, principle 17. A [[hybrid-broker]] can move you between books.\"},{\"kind\":\"order\",\"title\":\"Rank the five\",\"prompt\":\"Five made-up brokers. Rank them from your first choice to your last.\",\"items\":[\"A · EU licence on the register, STP with commission, segregated money, negative balance protection\",\"C · EU licence, declared B-book, good execution statistics, wider spreads\",\"E · Licensed ECN, high commissions, many complaints about slow withdrawals\",\"B · Offshore only, 500:1 leverage, 100% deposit bonus\",\"D · Unregulated 'managed account', 8% a month guaranteed\"],\"explain\":\"Licence, segregation and protections come first, and A and C both pass. A ranks first only because it doesn't deal against you; C could still win on costs. E's withdrawal complaints outweigh its licence. B and D are out: offshore bait, and a guaranteed return, the classic scam line.\",\"wrong\":\"Start with what protects your money: a licence you verified, segregation, negative balance protection. Red flags go last.\",\"concept\":\"broker-due-diligence\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"red\",\"label\":\"Red flag\"},{\"id\":\"weigh\",\"label\":\"Conflict or cost to weigh\"},{\"id\":\"good\",\"label\":\"Good sign\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"a1\",\"text\":\"Broker A · Licence number, legal name and website all match the regulator's register.\",\"answer\":\"good\",\"correct\":\"Right. A verified licence for the exact company you'd sign with: the first check passed.\",\"rationales\":{\"red\":\"Nothing here is a warning: every detail matches the register.\",\"weigh\":\"A verified licence carries no conflict and no extra cost. It's the first box ticked.\"}},{\"id\":\"b1\",\"text\":\"Broker B · Registered offshore only, yet it signs up EU residents.\",\"answer\":\"red\",\"correct\":\"Right. Offshore-only means no EU protections for you. Red flag one.\",\"rationales\":{\"weigh\":\"It isn't a cost you can weigh: without an EU licence, retail protections don't apply.\",\"good\":\"Signing up EU residents without an EU licence is the problem itself.\"}},{\"id\":\"b2\",\"text\":\"Broker B · Leverage up to 500:1.\",\"answer\":\"red\",\"correct\":\"Right. Over sixteen times the EU retail cap on major pairs. Red flag two.\",\"rationales\":{\"weigh\":\"No EU-regulated broker may offer this to retail clients. It isn't a trade-off.\",\"good\":\"More leverage isn't a feature here: it signals a firm outside retail rules.\"}},{\"id\":\"b3\",\"text\":\"Broker B · 100% bonus on your first deposit.\",\"answer\":\"red\",\"correct\":\"Right. Bonuses are banned for EU retail clients and exist to make you trade more. Red flag three.\",\"rationales\":{\"weigh\":\"A bonus isn't a discount to weigh: it's banned for EU retail clients and comes with trading conditions.\",\"good\":\"Free money is bait. EU rules ban bonuses for retail CFD clients.\"}},{\"id\":\"c1\",\"text\":\"Broker C · States that it is a B-book market maker and publishes execution statistics.\",\"answer\":\"weigh\",\"correct\":\"Right. A disclosed conflict with published data: weigh it, don't ignore it.\",\"rationales\":{\"red\":\"Disclosure and data are the opposite of a red flag. The conflict exists, but it's declared.\",\"good\":\"The transparency is good, but the dealing conflict is still there to weigh.\"}},{\"id\":\"d1\",\"text\":\"Broker D · Unregulated 'managed account' paying 8% a month, guaranteed.\",\"answer\":\"red\",\"correct\":\"Right. No market return is guaranteed; 8% a month is the classic scam line. Red flag four.\",\"rationales\":{\"weigh\":\"A guarantee isn't a cost to weigh. No one can promise market returns.\",\"good\":\"8% a month, guaranteed, is a promise no regulated firm may make.\"}},{\"id\":\"e1\",\"text\":\"Broker E · ECN pricing with a high commission per trade.\",\"answer\":\"weigh\",\"correct\":\"Right. A cost to compare against how often you trade, not a warning sign.\",\"rationales\":{\"red\":\"High fees are a cost, not misconduct. Compare them with the others.\",\"good\":\"ECN pricing is clean, but the commission is high: weigh it.\"}},{\"id\":\"e2\",\"text\":\"Broker E · Many recent complaints that withdrawals take months.\",\"answer\":\"red\",\"correct\":\"Right. A licence doesn't make withdrawals work. Red flag five.\",\"rationales\":{\"weigh\":\"Slow withdrawals aren't a cost: your money may not come back.\",\"good\":\"A licence is good, but many withdrawal complaints outweigh it.\"}},{\"id\":\"a2\",\"text\":\"Broker A · Client money segregated; negative balance protection for retail clients.\",\"answer\":\"good\",\"correct\":\"Right. Your money is kept apart, and the account can't go below zero.\",\"rationales\":{\"red\":\"Both are protections, not warnings.\",\"weigh\":\"These are protections for you, with no conflict attached.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Nine facts from the five brokers' files. Sort each one: a red flag, a conflict or cost to weigh, or a good sign.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":9},\"correct\":\"Done. All five red flags: offshore, 500:1, the bonus, the guaranteed return and slow withdrawals. Only A and C belong on a shortlist.\",\"hints\":[\"Ask first: who regulates the firm, and does the register confirm it?\",\"Guaranteed returns and deposit bonuses are red flags on their own.\",\"Five of the nine facts are red flags.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":9},\"concept\":\"broker-due-diligence\"},\"check\":[\"q-4.5.2-1\",\"q-4.5.2-2\",\"q-4.5.2-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Segregation protects your money; compensation schemes pay when firms fail, up to limits, never for market losses.\",\"A-book earns on your activity, B-book on your losses; a hybrid routes the winners out.\",\"Shortlist only verified, segregated, protected brokers; offshore, bonuses, guarantees and withdrawal complaints are red flags.\"]}},\"4.5.3\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.3\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"A trader with a real +0.2R edge per trade takes a challenge: +10% target, −10% maximum loss. Which risk per trade passes most often?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"r05\",\"label\":\"0.5%\"},{\"id\":\"r1\",\"label\":\"1%\"},{\"id\":\"r2\",\"label\":\"2%\"},{\"id\":\"r3\",\"label\":\"3%\"}],\"answer\":\"r05\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. In the lesson's simulation the +0.2R trader passes 84% of the time at 0.5% risk, 79% at 1%, 55% at 2% and 52% at 3%.\",\"concept\":\"prop-evaluation-rules\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Two businesses, one name\",\"text\":\"Institutional [[prop-firm|prop firms]] hire traders to trade the firm's own capital. Retail evaluation firms sell attempts: you pay a [[challenge-fee]] for an [[evaluation-challenge]] on a simulated account, and the [[funded-account]] you win is often still simulated, paying a [[profit-split]]. The second business sells tests.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"The rules, decoded\",\"prompt\":\"Match each rule with what it typically means.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"[[profit-target|Profit target]]\",\"right\":\"Gain 10%, then 5% in a second phase\"},{\"left\":\"Daily loss limit\",\"right\":\"5% of the initial balance, measured on equity with open trades\"},{\"left\":\"Static maximum loss\",\"right\":\"A fixed floor 10% below the initial balance\"},{\"left\":\"[[trailing-drawdown|Trailing drawdown]]\",\"right\":\"A floor that rises with your peak but never falls\"},{\"left\":\"[[consistency-rule|Consistency rule]]\",\"right\":\"Your best day under 50% of total profit\"}],\"columns\":[\"Rule\",\"Typical meaning\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Read every rule before paying: each one is a way to fail.\",\"concept\":\"prop-evaluation-rules\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Two examples · 2026, verify on the firms' sites\",\"text\":\"From third-party summaries: FTMO's 2-Step asks 10% then 5%, with a 5% daily and 10% static maximum loss, at least 4 trading days, and an 80% split that can reach 90%. Topstep's 50K futures account asks 3,000, with a 2,000 limit trailing the end-of-day balance and a consistency rule. Most firms also restrict news, weekends and copy trading.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"An intraday trailing floor\",\"lines\":[\"Account 50,000 vUSD; the limit trails your highest equity by 2,500.\",\"Start: the floor sits at 50,000 − 2,500 = 47,500.\",\"An open trade lifts equity to 51,500, open profit included.\",\"New floor: 51,500 − 2,500 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":49000,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":47500,\"text\":\"That's the static floor. An intraday trailing floor follows the peak of equity, even unrealised.\"},{\"value\":54000,\"text\":\"The floor sits below the peak: subtract 2,500 from 51,500.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 49,000. The floor rose 1,500 while the trade was still open.\"},\"after\":\"The trade then closes at break-even: equity 50,000, buffer only 1,000. Trailing the end-of-day balance instead, the floor would still be 47,500, since no day closed above 50,000.\",\"concept\":\"prop-evaluation-rules\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Risk per trade and the pass rate\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"+0.2R edge · 0.5% risk\",\"value\":84,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"+0.2R edge · 1% risk\",\"value\":79,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"+0.2R edge · 2% risk\",\"value\":55,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"No edge · 0.5% risk\",\"value\":12,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"No edge · 1% risk\",\"value\":31,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"No edge · 2% risk\",\"value\":31,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"−0.1R edge · 0.5% risk\",\"value\":0.3,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"−0.1R edge · 1% risk\",\"value\":5,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"−0.1R edge · 2% risk\",\"value\":15,\"role\":\"down\"}],\"steps\":[\"Edge +0.2R: 84%, 79%, 55%. Moderate risk passes most often.\",\"No edge: 12%, 31%, 31%. More risk helps, like a lottery.\",\"Losing −0.1R: 0.3%, 5%, 15%. Risk buys more lottery tickets.\"]},\"text\":\"Monte Carlo of a two-phase evaluation: +10% then +5%, a 5% daily and 10% static loss limit, 3 trades a day at a fixed risk, at most 60 days a phase. Each trader wins 40% of trades, at +2R, +1.5R or +1.25R. The figures depend on these assumptions.\",\"concept\":\"prop-evaluation-rules\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Without an edge, more risk raises the pass chance: you're buying a lottery ticket. With an edge, moderate risk wins. High risk also shortens your life in a funded account, where the same floors apply. Public pass rates, estimated at 5–10%, are unaudited.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Three floors, one day\",\"prompt\":\"Account 50,000, limit 2,500. On day 1 equity peaks at 51,900 during an open trade, and the day closes at 51,200. Where is each floor now?\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Static\",\"right\":\"47,500\",\"wrong\":\"A static floor never moves: 50,000 − 2,500.\"},{\"left\":\"Intraday trailing\",\"right\":\"49,400\",\"wrong\":\"It trails the highest equity, open profit included: 51,900 − 2,500.\"},{\"left\":\"End-of-day trailing\",\"right\":\"48,700\",\"wrong\":\"It trails the closing balance only: 51,200 − 2,500.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Floor type\",\"Floor after day 1\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Same day, three floors, three different buffers.\",\"concept\":\"prop-evaluation-rules\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Buffer left\",\"prompt\":\"Day 2 opens at 51,200 under the intraday trailing rule. How much can you lose before you hit the floor?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"answer\":1800,\"tolerance\":0,\"feedback\":[{\"value\":2500,\"text\":\"That's the full limit. The floor already rose to 49,400 with yesterday's peak.\"},{\"value\":3700,\"text\":\"That's the static floor's buffer. The trailing floor sits at 49,400.\"}]},\"reveal\":\"1,800: 51,200 − 49,400. The limit is 2,500, but the 700 of open profit given back yesterday already used part of it. Trailing floors punish giving back open gains.\",\"concept\":\"prop-evaluation-rules\"}],\"check\":[\"q-4.5.3-1\",\"q-4.5.3-2\",\"q-4.5.3-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Institutional prop firms trade their capital; retail evaluation firms sell tests, often on simulated accounts.\",\"Static floors stay put; intraday floors trail peak equity, end-of-day floors trail closing balances.\",\"Without an edge, more risk only buys lottery tickets; with one, moderate risk passes most often.\"]}},\"4.5.4\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.4\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"An evaluation costs 500. You estimate a 10% chance to pass and 1,500 of payouts on average if you get funded. What is the expected value?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"answer\":-350,\"tolerance\":1,\"feedback\":[{\"value\":1000,\"text\":\"That's payouts minus the fee, as if passing were certain. Weight the payouts by 10% first.\"},{\"value\":150,\"text\":\"That's the expected payout. The 500 fee is paid every time, pass or fail: subtract it.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You answered {yours}. EV = 0.10 × 1,500 − 500 = −350: on average, each attempt loses 350.\",\"concept\":\"prop-challenge-ev\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Expected value and break-even\",\"lines\":[\"[[challenge-expected-value|EV]] = P(pass) × expected payouts if funded − fee.\",\"0.10 × 1,500 − 500 = −350.\",\"Break-even: P × 1,500 = 500.\",\"P = 500 ÷ 1,500, as a percentage = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":33.33,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":0.33,\"text\":\"As a percentage: 0.333 × 100 = 33.3%.\"},{\"value\":300,\"text\":\"That's 1,500 ÷ 500. Put the fee on top: 500 ÷ 1,500.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 33.3%. You'd need to pass one attempt in three just to break even.\"},\"after\":\"If attempts are independent and your skill doesn't change, a 10% pass chance means 1 ÷ 0.10 = 10 expected attempts per funded account: 5,000 in fees.\",\"concept\":\"prop-challenge-ev\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"What the industry numbers say\",\"text\":\"Pass rates are estimated at 5–10%, or 2–20% depending on rules. Traders report 2–4 attempts before a first funded account, 800–1,600 dollars in fees. [[payout-rate|Payout rates]] are rarely published; claims like '7% get paid' are unverified. Fees from failed attempts fund the model, and many firms run a [[simulated-funded-account]] that never reaches a real market.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"The My Forex Funds case · as of 22 Oct 2026\",\"text\":\"In 2023 the CFTC sued Traders Global Group, trading as My Forex Funds: over 135,000 customers, at least $310 million in fees, and the firm allegedly acting as counterparty while claiming outside liquidity. In May 2025 the case was dismissed, with sanctions against the CFTC's enforcement team, according to the defence. Protection for fee payers is unclear.\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Evaluation rules are good discipline training, and the simulator gives them to you free. A bought evaluation is a cost with an expected value you can calculate, not a career. Several firms closed or changed their rules suddenly in 2024.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Estimate first\",\"prompt\":\"A trader's last 100 simulated trades, after costs: 38 wins of +1.5R, 62 losses of −1R. At 1% risk, how often would this trader pass the 4.5.3 evaluation? Within 3 points counts.\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":0,\"max\":60,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"start\":0,\"answer\":17,\"tolerance\":3,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":0,\"to\":13,\"text\":\"Higher than that: at 1% risk, lucky runs still carry a losing trader past the target fairly often.\"},{\"from\":21,\"to\":60,\"text\":\"Lower than that: the expectancy is −0.05R a trade, so most runs drift down to the floor.\"}]},\"reveal\":\"The engine resampled these 100 trades through the rules 400,000 times: about 17%. Expectancy is 0.38 × 1.5 − 0.62 = −0.05R, yet 1 run in 6 passes, through luck at 1% risk. At 0.5% risk it's about 4%: pass chance depends on risk, not only on edge.\",\"concept\":\"prop-challenge-ev\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The fee that breaks even\",\"lines\":[\"Pass probability from the engine: 17%.\",\"Payouts if funded, as before: 1,500.\",\"Break-even fee = 0.17 × 1,500 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":255,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":500,\"text\":\"That's the fee charged, not the fee that breaks even. Multiply the pass chance by the payouts.\"},{\"value\":1245,\"text\":\"That's 1,500 − 255. The break-even fee is the expected payout itself: 0.17 × 1,500.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 255. At a 500 fee, this trader's EV is 255 − 500 = −245 per attempt.\"},\"after\":\"Even at a fair fee, these trades lose 0.05R each: a pass would be luck, and the funded account would drift down too.\",\"concept\":\"prop-challenge-ev\"}],\"check\":[\"q-4.5.4-1\",\"q-4.5.4-2\",\"q-4.5.4-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"EV = pass probability × payouts − fee; break-even pass probability = fee ÷ payouts.\",\"Estimate your pass chance from your own trades and risk; without an edge, passes are luck.\",\"The My Forex Funds case shows evaluation buyers can't count on regulators to protect them.\"]}},\"4.5.5\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.5\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Two traders each make 10% a year before tax for 10 years, starting from 10,000. One pays a generic 20% tax on each year's gain. How much less does she end with?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":0,\"max\":8000,\"step\":50,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"start\":0,\"answer\":4350,\"tolerance\":300,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":0,\"to\":4000,\"text\":\"More than that: each year's tax also stops that money compounding in every later year.\"},{\"from\":4700,\"to\":8000,\"text\":\"Less than that: she still grows 8% a year, against 10%.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. 10,000 × 1.10¹⁰ = 25,937 against 10,000 × 1.08¹⁰ = 21,589: about 4,348 less.\",\"concept\":\"tax-awareness\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"This is not tax advice. Rules differ by country and change often. Ask your tax authority or a qualified adviser. The app gives no tax rates and calculates no tax for any country; the 20% in this lesson is a made-up round number.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Tax and compounding\",\"lines\":[\"No tax: 10,000 × 1.10¹⁰ = 25,937.42.\",\"A generic 20% on each year's gain turns 10% into 8%.\",\"Taxed every year: 10,000 × 1.08¹⁰ = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":21589.25,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":18000,\"text\":\"That adds 8% of 10,000 each year, without compounding. Multiply by 1.08 ten times instead.\"},{\"value\":25937.42,\"text\":\"That's the untaxed result. Use 8%, the rate left after a 20% tax on 10%.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 21,589.25, which is 4,348.17 less. The [[after-tax-return|after-tax return]] is what compounds.\"},\"after\":\"Tax comes out of every year's gain, so the gap grows with time. Compare strategies on what's left after costs and tax, not on gross returns.\",\"concept\":\"tax-awareness\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Why the rules differ · illustrative, check locally\",\"prompt\":\"Real examples as of Oct 2026, simplified. Match each with what it shows.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"US [[wash-sale-rule|wash-sale rule]]\",\"right\":\"Timing matters: rebuying within 30 days can disallow a loss\"},{\"left\":\"US Section 1256 futures\",\"right\":\"Instrument matters: gains taxed 60% as long-term, 40% as short-term\"},{\"left\":\"UK spread betting versus CFDs\",\"right\":\"Product matters: similar trades, but only one is usually subject to [[capital-gains-tax]]\"},{\"left\":\"Romania, local or foreign broker\",\"right\":\"Broker matters: tax [[withholding-tax|withheld at source]], or declared by you\"}],\"columns\":[\"Example\",\"What it shows\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Country, instrument, timing and broker all change the tax on the same trade. For your case, ask your tax authority or an adviser.\",\"concept\":\"tax-awareness\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"What to keep\",\"text\":\"Keep [[trade-records]] for every trade: date and time, instrument, size, entry and exit prices, commissions, swap or [[overnight-financing|financing]], and the exchange rate used to convert the result into your home currency. Keep broker statements and every deposit and withdrawal too. How long you must keep them differs by country; several years is common.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Record or journal?\",\"prompt\":\"Sort each item: does it belong in the trade record you keep for tax and review, or only in your journal?\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Commission on entry and on exit\",\"right\":\"Trade record\",\"wrong\":\"Fees change the taxable result: they belong in the record.\"},{\"left\":\"Overnight financing charged\",\"right\":\"Trade record\",\"wrong\":\"Financing is a cost of the trade, with a date and an amount: record it.\"},{\"left\":\"Exchange rate on the trade date\",\"right\":\"Trade record\",\"wrong\":\"Without the rate, nobody can check the converted result.\"},{\"left\":\"A 500.00 withdrawal to your bank\",\"right\":\"Trade record\",\"wrong\":\"Every cash movement goes in the record, not only trades.\"},{\"left\":\"How you felt before the entry\",\"right\":\"Journal only\",\"wrong\":\"Feelings matter for review, but they're not a money fact: journal only.\"},{\"left\":\"A screenshot of the setup\",\"right\":\"Journal only\",\"wrong\":\"A chart helps your review. The record holds dates and amounts.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Item\",\"Where it goes\"],\"correct\":\"Right. The record holds every money fact with a date; the [[journal]] adds why you traded and how you felt.\",\"concept\":\"trade-records\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Into your home currency\",\"lines\":[\"Bought 50 LABX at 97.30, sold at 102.40: gross +255.00 vUSD.\",\"Commissions: 2.44 in, 2.56 out. Net +250.00 vUSD.\",\"Rate on the trade date: 4.60 local units per vUSD.\",\"Net in local currency: 250.00 × 4.60 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":1150,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"local units\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":1173,\"text\":\"That converts the gross 255.00. Fees are part of the record: convert the net 250.00.\"},{\"value\":54.35,\"text\":\"That divides. The rate is local units per vUSD, so multiply.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 1,150.00. Record the rate and its source with the trade.\"},\"after\":\"Use the rate of the trade date, from a source your tax authority accepts. A record missing fees or the rate can't be checked later.\",\"concept\":\"trade-records\"}],\"check\":[\"q-4.5.5-1\",\"q-4.5.5-2\",\"q-4.5.5-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Record every trade, fee, financing charge, exchange rate, deposit and withdrawal, with dates.\",\"A generic 20% tax on each year's gain turns 10% into 8%: 4,348 less after 10 years.\",\"Tax differs by country, product, timing and broker. Ask your tax authority or a qualified adviser.\"]}},\"4.5.6\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.6\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Your simulator results: 60 trades, +0.35R average, 98% stop integrity. Ready to go live?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Yes, the edge is clear\",\"feedback\":\"An average from 60 trades is noisy. Check the interval around it first.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Not yet: the interval still includes zero\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Yes, but with 2% risk\",\"feedback\":\"More size makes it worse: the evidence is the problem, not the size.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only if you feel confident\",\"feedback\":\"Confidence isn't evidence. A written checklist decides, not a feeling.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. With a 1.5R standard deviation, the 95% interval runs from −0.03R to +0.73R: zero is still inside.\",\"concept\":\"go-live-criteria\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The interval decides\",\"lines\":[\"60 trades, average +0.35R, standard deviation 1.5R.\",\"[[standard-error|Standard error]]: 1.5 ÷ √60 = 0.194.\",\"Half-width: 1.96 × 0.194 = 0.38.\",\"Lower bound: 0.35 − 0.38 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":-0.03,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":0.16,\"text\":\"That subtracts one standard error. A 95% interval reaches 1.96 standard errors: 0.38.\"},{\"value\":-1.15,\"text\":\"That subtracts the spread of single trades. Use the standard error of the average.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: −0.03R. The interval runs from −0.03R to +0.73R.\"},\"after\":\"Zero is inside, so the data can't yet rule out no edge. More trades narrow the [[confidence-interval]]; one that excludes zero is the first gate.\",\"concept\":\"expectancy-confidence-interval\"},{\"kind\":\"order\",\"title\":\"The go-live checklist\",\"prompt\":\"Put the six parts of the [[go-live-checklist]] in the order you check them.\",\"items\":[\"Evidence: a validation dossier and 50+ forward-test trades inside the Monte Carlo band\",\"Process: stop integrity 95%+, risk accuracy and plan adherence 90%+, limits kept 100%\",\"Capital: risk capital only, emergency savings apart, nothing borrowed\",\"Broker: licence verified on the register, money segregated, negative balance protection\",\"Plan: minimum live size, written kill criteria, a premortem\",\"Expectations: live results will be worse than the simulator's\"],\"explain\":\"Evidence first: without a validated edge, nothing else matters. Process shows you can execute it. Capital and broker decide whether trying is safe. The plan limits the damage, and expectations prepare you for the [[sim-to-live-gap]].\",\"wrong\":\"Start with the evidence: no edge, no go. End with what you expect once real money is at stake.\",\"note\":{\"key\":\"go-live-gap\",\"label\":\"Which part would you fail today, and what would fix it?\",\"placeholder\":\"e.g. Evidence: only 40 forward-test trades so far; 10 more weeks in the simulator\"},\"concept\":\"go-live-criteria\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The business case: fixed costs and income\",\"lines\":[\"Risk capital: 10,000.00 vUSD.\",\"Platform 45.00 + market data 15.00 = 60.00 a month, 720.00 a year.\",\"Hurdle: 720.00 ÷ 10,000.00 = 7.2% a year before you earn anything.\",\"To draw 1,500.00 a month at an optimistic 20% a year after all costs, you'd need capital of ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":90000,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":7500,\"text\":\"That's one month's income ÷ 0.20. Use the yearly income: 1,500.00 × 12 = 18,000.00.\"},{\"value\":3600,\"text\":\"That's 20% of 18,000.00. Divide instead: the capital must earn 18,000.00 at 20%.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right: 18,000.00 ÷ 0.20 = 90,000.00, nine times this account.\"},\"after\":\"A small account can't pay a salary, and fixed costs eat a large share of it. Start as a side activity at [[minimum-live-size]], with income from elsewhere.\",\"concept\":\"go-live-criteria\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Why live is worse\",\"text\":\"Expect live results to be worse than simulated ones: real spreads and [[slippage]], partial fills, outages and, above all, fear and greed with your own money. That [[sim-to-live-gap]] is normal. The simulator builds skill; it doesn't predict real profits. Plan for a smaller edge live, and size for it.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Three valid answers\",\"text\":\"The checklist ends in one of three decisions. **Live at minimum size**: every part passes. **Not yet**: something fixable is missing, such as forward-test trades or a verified broker. **Don't trade live**: honest testing found no edge after costs, or there's no money you can afford to lose. All three are good outcomes of this course.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"live\",\"label\":\"Live at minimum size\"},{\"id\":\"notyet\",\"label\":\"Not yet\"},{\"id\":\"dont\",\"label\":\"Don't trade live\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"text\":\"Profile A · 60 trades, interval −0.03R to +0.73R, KPIs on target. The only money available is the deposit saved for a flat next year.\",\"answer\":\"dont\",\"correct\":\"Right. Two parts fail: zero is inside the interval, and money needed next year isn't risk capital. No live trading with it.\",\"rationales\":{\"live\":\"Two parts fail: zero is inside the interval, and a home deposit due next year isn't risk capital.\",\"notyet\":\"More trades could fix the evidence, not the money. Savings needed within a year never become risk capital.\"}},{\"id\":\"b\",\"text\":\"Profile B · Dossier verdict: deploy to forward test. 120 forward trades inside the Monte Carlo band, KPIs above thresholds, risk capital set aside, licence verified, kill criteria written.\",\"answer\":\"live\",\"correct\":\"Right. Evidence, process, capital, broker and plan all pass. Start at minimum size, and expect live to be worse.\",\"rationales\":{\"notyet\":\"Every part passes: evidence, KPIs, capital, broker and a written plan. Waiting adds nothing.\",\"dont\":\"There's a validated edge and risk capital. The checklist says live, at minimum size.\"}},{\"id\":\"c\",\"text\":\"Profile C · KPIs above thresholds, risk capital set aside, licence verified. The system has a backtest only: no validation dossier, no forward test.\",\"answer\":\"notyet\",\"correct\":\"Right. The discipline is there, but the edge isn't validated. Build the dossier and forward test first.\",\"rationales\":{\"live\":\"Good discipline without a validated edge isn't enough: the evidence part is empty.\",\"dont\":\"Nothing shows the edge is absent; it's untested. That's fixable: not yet.\"}},{\"id\":\"d\",\"text\":\"Profile D · Two systems, 300 forward trades each, KPIs excellent. After costs, neither beats the passive benchmark.\",\"answer\":\"dont\",\"correct\":\"Right. Honest testing found no edge after costs. Not trading live, and investing passively instead, is a good result.\",\"rationales\":{\"live\":\"Discipline is excellent, but neither system beats the passive benchmark after costs.\",\"notyet\":\"Six hundred forward trades is a real test. When the answer is no edge after costs, waiting won't change it.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Four trader profiles. For each, decide: live at minimum size, not yet, or don't trade live.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":4},\"correct\":\"Done: one live, one not yet, two don't. Each decision rests on at least two parts of the checklist.\",\"hints\":[\"Capital you need for life plans is not risk capital.\",\"Good discipline without a validated edge is not enough.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":4},\"concept\":\"go-live-criteria\"},\"check\":[\"q-4.5.6-1\",\"q-4.5.6-2\",\"q-4.5.6-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Go live only when evidence, process, capital, broker, plan and expectations all pass the written checklist.\",\"The confidence interval decides, not the average: if it includes zero, the answer is not yet.\",\"Live results are worse than simulated: costs, slippage and real emotions. Start at minimum size.\"]}},\"4.5.7\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.7\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Four mornings. On which one should you trade?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"You hit your daily loss limit yesterday and want to win it back today\",\"feedback\":\"Wanting to win it back is how revenge trading starts. Stand aside until the urge has gone.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The market looks like noise and none of your setups appear\",\"feedback\":\"No setup means no trade. In noise, staying out is the skill.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A tier-1 release is due in 10 minutes and your plan has no rule for it\",\"feedback\":\"No rule for the event means standing aside until it has passed.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"You feel calm and a setup from your playbook appears\",\"feedback\":\"Right. You're in a fit state, and the setup is one you've tested.\"}],\"answer\":\"d\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. Only the calm morning with a playbook setup is a trading day. The other three are reasons to stand aside.\",\"concept\":\"no-trade-conditions\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Four families of no-trade states\",\"prompt\":\"Sort each state into its family.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Tilt after a loss\",\"right\":\"Personal\",\"wrong\":\"Tilt is about you, not the market: a personal state.\"},{\"left\":\"Fever or a sleepless night\",\"right\":\"Personal\",\"wrong\":\"Illness and fatigue are personal states that weaken decisions.\"},{\"left\":\"Noise, with none of your setups\",\"right\":\"Market\",\"wrong\":\"The market offers no edge you can trade: a market state.\"},{\"left\":\"Thin holiday liquidity\",\"right\":\"Market\",\"wrong\":\"Thin volume and wide spreads are market conditions.\"},{\"left\":\"Weekly loss limit reached\",\"right\":\"Process\",\"wrong\":\"A limit your plan sets is a process rule.\"},{\"left\":\"A setup that isn't in your playbook\",\"right\":\"Process\",\"wrong\":\"Your playbook defines what you trade: a process rule.\"},{\"left\":\"Money you'll need within two years\",\"right\":\"Financial\",\"wrong\":\"That's about your money, not your trading: a financial state.\"},{\"left\":\"No emergency fund\",\"right\":\"Financial\",\"wrong\":\"Without an emergency fund, any trading money is at risk of being needed: financial.\"}],\"columns\":[\"State\",\"Family\"],\"correct\":\"Right. Check all four, you, the market, your process and your money, before you look for a trade.\",\"concept\":\"no-trade-conditions\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Write it before the day\",\"text\":\"A [[stand-aside-rule]] turns each state into a written condition: 'after the daily limit, no new trades until tomorrow'; 'a tier-1 release without a rule: no new trades from 15 minutes before'. Mark a [[no-trade-day]] in advance at your weekly review. Rules written when calm beat willpower in the moment.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Start from the base rate\",\"prompt\":\"Before you have any live record of your own, what should you assume about your odds of trading profitably?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Better than even: I've trained for this\",\"feedback\":\"Most people in these statistics believed that too. Training isn't evidence of an edge.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Close to the base rate: most active traders lose\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing: they can't be estimated\",\"feedback\":\"They can: whole populations of retail traders have been measured. Start there.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"reveal\":\"Start from the [[base-rate]]: 74–89% of EU retail CFD accounts lose (ESMA); 97% of Brazilians who day traded futures for over 300 days lost; under 1% of Taiwanese day traders profit predictably; the most active fifth of US households earned 11.4% a year against the market's 17.9%. Only validated, after-cost evidence of your own should move you.\",\"concept\":\"base-rates\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"The default alternative\",\"text\":\"If active trading doesn't earn its place, the usual alternative is diversified, low-cost [[buy-and-hold|passive investing]]: the [[benchmark]] every result here is measured against. Deciding not to day trade, or not to trade real money at all, is a valid outcome of this course, not a failure. This isn't investment advice; it's the comparison every plan must pass.\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Abstaining is scored. In the Arena, staying out of no-edge stretches such as R0 noise raises your [[abstention-accuracy]], and cutting exposure in a crisis your plan doesn't cover counts as a correct decision. A day without trades can be your best-played day.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"bias-spotter\",\"data\":{\"options\":[{\"id\":\"trade\",\"label\":\"Trade\"},{\"id\":\"reduce\",\"label\":\"Reduce size\"},{\"id\":\"aside\",\"label\":\"Stand aside\"}],\"cards\":[{\"id\":\"d1\",\"text\":\"Day 1 · You slept well, your pre-trade checklist is green, and a playbook setup forms at your level.\",\"answer\":\"trade\",\"correct\":\"Right. Fit state, intact limits, a tested setup: a trading day.\",\"rationales\":{\"reduce\":\"No rule calls for a smaller size: state, limits and setup are all fine.\",\"aside\":\"Nothing here calls for standing aside. Abstaining without a reason is a missed trade.\"}},{\"id\":\"d2\",\"text\":\"Day 2 · Yesterday you hit your daily loss limit. This morning you want to make it back fast.\",\"answer\":\"aside\",\"correct\":\"Right. The urge to win it back is a personal state on top of a process one. No trades today.\",\"rationales\":{\"trade\":\"Wanting to make it back is the start of revenge trading. Stand aside.\",\"reduce\":\"A smaller size doesn't fix the urge to win it back. Stand aside today.\"}},{\"id\":\"d3\",\"text\":\"Day 3 · No setup from your playbook all morning; the chart is choppy noise.\",\"answer\":\"aside\",\"correct\":\"Right. No setup, no trade. In noise every trade costs its costs.\",\"rationales\":{\"trade\":\"No playbook setup appeared. A trade here has no edge.\",\"reduce\":\"Size isn't the issue: there's no setup to size.\"}},{\"id\":\"d4\",\"text\":\"Day 4 · Your first drawdown tier was hit last week; the rule says half size until a new equity high. A valid setup appears.\",\"answer\":\"reduce\",\"correct\":\"Right. The setup is valid, and the tier rule sets the size: half.\",\"rationales\":{\"trade\":\"The tier rule still applies: half size until a new equity high.\",\"aside\":\"The first tier cuts size; it doesn't stop trading. The setup is valid.\"}},{\"id\":\"d5\",\"text\":\"Day 5 · A tier-1 release is due in 10 minutes, and your plan has no rule for it.\",\"answer\":\"aside\",\"correct\":\"Right. No rule for the event: stand aside until it has passed.\",\"rationales\":{\"trade\":\"Without a rule for the release, the outcome is a coin flip with wide spreads.\",\"reduce\":\"A smaller size still trades an event you have no plan for. Stand aside.\"}},{\"id\":\"d6\",\"text\":\"Day 6 · Volatility has doubled. Your setup is valid, but the stop must sit twice as far away.\",\"answer\":\"reduce\",\"correct\":\"Right. Same risk, twice the stop distance: half the units.\",\"rationales\":{\"trade\":\"Full units with twice the stop would double the risk. Size from the stop.\",\"aside\":\"The setup is valid. Sizing from the stop handles the volatility.\"}},{\"id\":\"d7\",\"text\":\"Day 7 · Christmas Eve: volume is a fraction of normal and spreads are wide.\",\"answer\":\"aside\",\"correct\":\"Right. Thin holiday liquidity is a market state to stand aside in.\",\"rationales\":{\"trade\":\"Thin volume and wide spreads make costs and slippage unpredictable.\",\"reduce\":\"The costs per trade rise whatever the size. Stand aside.\"}},{\"id\":\"d8\",\"text\":\"Day 8 · A family emergency is on your mind; you've checked your phone ten times in an hour.\",\"answer\":\"aside\",\"correct\":\"Right. A distracted mind is a personal state to stand aside in.\",\"rationales\":{\"trade\":\"Your attention is elsewhere. Execution and stop discipline suffer.\",\"reduce\":\"A smaller size with a distracted mind is still a bad trade.\"}},{\"id\":\"d9\",\"text\":\"Day 9 · Weekly review done, limits intact, and the setup you've traded 200 times in the simulator appears.\",\"answer\":\"trade\",\"correct\":\"Right. Everything checks out: take the trade at your normal size.\",\"rationales\":{\"reduce\":\"No rule calls for a smaller size here.\",\"aside\":\"Every family checks out. Standing aside would miss a valid trade.\"}},{\"id\":\"d10\",\"text\":\"Day 10 · A crisis moves the market in ways your plan doesn't cover; you hold one position.\",\"answer\":\"reduce\",\"correct\":\"Right. Cut exposure when the plan doesn't cover the regime.\",\"rationales\":{\"trade\":\"Adding risk in a regime your plan doesn't cover is guessing.\",\"aside\":\"You already hold a position. Cutting it down is the decision; take no new trades.\"}},{\"id\":\"d11\",\"text\":\"Day 11 · Rent is due next week, and part of it sits in your trading account.\",\"answer\":\"aside\",\"correct\":\"Right. Money you need next week isn't risk capital. Take it out first.\",\"rationales\":{\"trade\":\"Trading money you need for rent risks your living costs.\",\"reduce\":\"Any size risks money that isn't risk capital. Stand aside.\"}},{\"id\":\"d12\",\"text\":\"Day 12 · After a calm loss within your plan, you take a 10-minute break, and a fresh playbook setup appears.\",\"answer\":\"trade\",\"correct\":\"Right. A planned loss and a break leave you fit. The new setup is valid.\",\"rationales\":{\"reduce\":\"One loss inside the plan doesn't trigger a size rule.\",\"aside\":\"A loss within the plan isn't tilt. The break did its job.\"}}]},\"goal\":\"Twelve day cards. For each, choose: trade, reduce size, or stand aside. Aim for 10 or more right on the first try.\",\"success\":{\"field\":\"solved\",\"eq\":12},\"correct\":\"Twelve days sorted: three trades, three smaller sizes, six days aside. The widget shows how many you got on the first try.\",\"hints\":[\"Check your process state before the chart.\",\"No setup means no trade.\",\"Reduce size when a written rule or a wider stop says so.\"],\"showMe\":{\"solved\":12},\"concept\":\"no-trade-conditions\"},\"check\":[\"q-4.5.7-1\",\"q-4.5.7-2\",\"q-4.5.7-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Stand aside on personal, market, process or financial states, written as rules before the day.\",\"Start from base rates: most active retail traders lose. Only validated evidence should move you.\",\"Abstaining when there's no edge is a scored skill, and not trading live is a valid outcome.\"]}}}");
